  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Rhizina undulata, commonly known as Doughnut Fungus, is a soil-dwelling fungus in the Rhizinaceae family that forms distinctive cup-shaped fruiting bodies. This species is notable for causing root rot in conifers and appearing in disturbed soil areas throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cup-shaped fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Forms brown, wavy-edged cups or discs that resemble doughnuts, typically 1-4 cm in diameter",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Whitish root-like strands",
      "description": "Produces conspicuous mycelial cords that extend from the fruiting body into surrounding soil",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Preference for disturbed soil",
      "description": "Commonly appears in mulched areas, recently burned sites, and around conifer plantations",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Doughnut Fungus thrives in disturbed soil, mulch piles, and areas around conifers in New England forests and gardens, particularly in acidic soils.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its appealing doughnut-like appearance, Rhizina undulata is a pathogenic fungus that can cause serious root rot in economically important conifers like spruce and fir.",
